@mainpage Peano

@image html documentation/Peano-logo.png

\tableofcontents


Peano is an open source framework for solvers on dynamically adaptive Cartesian meshes.
It is written in C++, but many tools around it are Python.
Peano is based upon the fact that spacetrees, a generalisation of the
classical octree concept, yield a cascade of adaptive Cartesian grids.
Consequently, any spacetree traversal is equivalent to an element-wise
traversal of the hierarchy of the adaptive Cartesian grids. The software
Peano  realises such a grid traversal and storage algorithm, and it provides
hook-in points for applications performing per-element, per-vertex, and so
forth operations on the grid. It also provides interfaces for dynamic load
balancing, sophisticated geometry representations, and other features.

On top of Peano, we have created multiple extensions which use this meshing infrastructure for particular application domains.
Examples are extensions for SPH (@ref page_swift2_home), hyperbolic solvers (@ref page_exahype2_home) or multigrid (@ref page_multigrid_home).
The @ref page_architecture_home "architecture page" provides an overview including some illustrations.
Again, the extensions are kind of tools that allow you to write simulations for your particular domain.
They are typically not "ready to use" products, even though some of these applications can almost be used as such.

<br /><br />
<div style="background-color: #cfc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
Peano (with any extension) is <b>not</b> a code. It is a tool to build your code. 
However, some of the applications built on top of Peano with all its toolboxes and extensions provide "ready to use" options.
All components are integrated into one repository, one code base, and documented on these pages.
</div>





